<h2>Not yet read</h2>
<p>One item, new. The page holds only what you have not read. At 04:22 UTC on 6 November, you marked the page read up to change 32, so the new item is numbered 33.</p>
<p>Twice in two batches, the ledger tool refused a note that a batch&rsquo;s agent tried to add to a ledger row, because the note was too long. The tool now has a check that says whether a note fits before it is written, and the skill tells the workers of it. A cold read of the first wording then found passages that would make a worker search, and the skill writer fixed them. The item shows the net change of both rounds.</p>
<p>The item shows the text on <code>main</code> now, against the text before the first of the two commits. The words that were added are underlined.</p>

<section id="l33" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 33 &middot; records.md, &ldquo;Writing to the record&rdquo;</span>A note&rsquo;s length checked before it is written</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what was added</span>
<p class="where-in">records.md, &ldquo;Writing to the record&rdquo;, &ldquo;To change a row&rdquo;, line 105, the first bullet</p>
<ul><li><code>$L note N TEXT</code> adds TEXT to the notes of row N. It refuses a note that takes the row past 1,200 characters, or its notes cell past 700 characters. <span class="add"><code>$L note N TEXT --check</code> writes nothing. It says whether TEXT fits, and how many characters the notes and the row have left now and would have left with TEXT. These counts include the separator that the tool adds. It exits 0 if TEXT fits, and 1 if not. Run it before a long note, or before a note on a row near its limits. If TEXT does not fit, shorten it, or cut the notes with <code>rewrite</code> (below).</span></li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The bullet gave the two limits of a ledger row, but no way to test a note against them before writing it, and the ledger tool refused notes of batch agents for their length twice in two batches. It now gives the tool&rsquo;s check, which writes nothing and says whether the note fits. The first wording squeezed the counts that the check prints into a few words, left out its exit status, said to run it only before a note that may be long, and did not say what to do with a note that does not fit. After the cold read, the bullet says what the counts are, and that they include the separator that the tool adds, so a note of one character costs two. It gives the exit status, for a worker that scripts the check. It says to run the check also before a note on a row near its limits, since such a row refuses even a short note. And it says what to do when the note does not fit: shorten it, or cut the row&rsquo;s notes with <code>rewrite</code>.</p></div>
</section>

